{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 5, :comments ["<b>And if she gives birth to a female.</b> The reason it was necessary to say <b>תֵלֵד</b> and did not attach it to what it mentioned earlier, ״וְיָלְדָה״ — they said in Torat Kohanim, and this is their language: I have only [the case of] a female; from where do I include a tumtum and an androgynos? Scripture says ״וְאִם נְקֵבָה תֵלֵד״ — the matter depends only on the birth. Thus far. And I do not dispute their words, heaven forbid, but rather as an addition, for from His saying ״וְאִם נְקֵבָה״ and not saying ״וְכִי תֵלֵד נְקֵבָה״ or ״וְלִנְקֵבָה״ etc., He intended to say: even if she is not definitively a female but [rather] ״וְאִם נְקֵבָה,״ meaning a doubt — and if so, tumtum and androgynos are included, for they are a doubt, and it would not have been necessary to say ״תֵלֵד.״" "Or perhaps, when it said ״תֵלֵד״ it was to include the androgynos, according to the one who says (Bekhorot 4:2) that it is a creation unto itself — for this would not have been derived from the phrase ״וְאִם נְקֵבָה,״ since it has a definite aspect of maleness." "Or perhaps, the Tanna of Torat Kohanim holds as follows: he includes the tumtum from the phrase ״וְאִם,״ and the androgynos from the inclusion of ״תֵלֵד,״ and he holds like the one who says it is a creation unto itself. Therefore he said: Scripture says ״וְאִם נְקֵבָה תֵלֵד,״ and it did not suffice for him to say Scripture says ״תֵלֵד.״" "But one must settle the verse according to the one who says the androgynos is a doubt: why was it necessary to say ״תֵּלֵד״? Perhaps it is that even according to the one who says it is a doubt, a single derivation would not have sufficed for both of them, for the reason that the doubt of a tumtum is a doubt that can be resolved and it may be found to be a female, and therefore one must be concerned about it. Whereas the doubt of an androgynos stands as such forever, and therefore two inclusions were necessary. And regarding what they said at the beginning of the last chapter of Yoma (Yoma 4a): that a verse does not come for a doubt, because before Heaven it is revealed whether it is male or female — if so, for a tumtum it would follow that its impurity as a female is only rabbinic. From now on, the androgynos too is only rabbinic, from the fact that the Tanna equated them together: just as the tumtum is nothing but an asmakhta, so too the androgynos is only rabbinic and they relied on an asmakhta. And for stringency we go with it and not for leniency, as they said in the chapter Yesh Nochalin (Bava Batra 127a): for that which was taught — one who miscarries a tumtum or an androgynos, she sits [the days of impurity] for a male and for a female. Thus far — meaning, the days of purity of a male and the days of impurity of a female, as a stringency taking the stricter of both; see their words there. And according to this, the verse's saying ״וְאִם נְקֵבָה תֵלֵד,״ by which we include the cases of doubt, is only to connect it to what follows, namely ״וְטָמְאָה שְׁבֻעַיִם.״ But her days of purity are only like the days of a male, so that if she saw blood from the fortieth day after her delivery, she has no status of blood of purity."], :book :leviticus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 12, :notes {3 "The Torat Kohanan's second derivation appears to state that the single phrase 'תַּלְמוּד לוֹמַר' applies to both words together; translated hyper-literally preserving the awkward syntax of the original."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:55:53.761177197Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}